This week in I&S, I learned about constitutional monarchy and the difference between formal authority and actual political power. We looked at how a constitutional monarchy works, how it is different from an absolute monarchy, and what strengths and weaknesses this system can have.
One important idea I learned is that someone can be the head of state without controlling the day-to-day government. In a constitutional monarchy, the monarch’s power is limited by constitutional rules, while elected institutions and the government hold most of the political power.
I also learned important political vocabulary such as non-partisan, public funds, head of state, constitution, symbolic, and hereditary. These words helped me understand that political systems are not only about who has a title, but also about what kind of power they actually have.
This activity also helped me practise taking notes quickly because we only had ten minutes and were only allowed to pause the video once. I had to focus on the most important information and then use it in Cornell Notes with my group.
The main idea I took from this lesson is that having an important position does not always mean having the most political power.
